-- title:
--   Polynomials in j with non-zero reduction are R₀-units
-- statement:
--   Fix a prime $q$ with $q \ge 5$, a non-zero natural number $M'$ not divisible by $q$, and a valuation subring $A$ of $\overline{\mathbb Q}$ lying over $q$ in the sense that $q$ is a non-unit of $A$. Let $R_0$ be a `ConstantReduction` datum for $A$ relating the field $\overline{\mathbb Q}$-generated by the coefficientwise images of the full modular function field of level $M'$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ to the field $\kappa(A)(\mathrm{jqModC}, \mathrm{jqNModC}_{M'})$ inside $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\kappa(A))$: that is, a valuation subring $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, a surjective ring homomorphism $R_0.\mathrm{residue}$ onto the latter field with kernel the maximal ideal, membership over $A$ and compatibility with $\kappa(A)$ along $A$, a scaling property, together with a degree-preserving, order-compatible map on places. Assume the pinning hypothesis $hR_0$: every Laurent series $y$ with coefficients in $A$ whose coefficientwise image in $\mathrm{LaurentSeries}(\overline{\mathbb Q})$ lies in that field belongs to $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and has $R_0$-residue equal, as a Laurent series over $\kappa(A)$, to the coefficientwise reduction of $y$. Then for every polynomial $P$ over $A$ whose coefficientwise reduction in $\kappa(A)[X]$ is non-zero, the value of $P$ at the element given by the coefficientwise image of the $q$-expansion $\mathrm{jq}$ of $j$ lies in $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and so does its inverse.
--
--   This is the "Gauss point" statement for the constant-reduction ring $R_0$ attached to the full modular function field of level $M'$: polynomials in $j$ over $A$ with non-zero reduction are units of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$, since the residue of such a value is the reduced polynomial evaluated at $q^{-1} + \cdots$. It is used in the identification of $R_0.\mathrm{integers}$ and of its maximal ideal in terms of $q$-expansions, and in the level-structure computations for the semistable specialisation of the modular curve.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_ModularCurve_FullLevel_aeval_jq_mem_integers_and_inv_mem_of_map_residue_ne_zero.lean

